” in New Fiction Journal 1 (August 2010). (Short Story)<br>228.    “Musings from an Infant`s Face” in Labyrinth: The Biannual Literary Journal of Postmodernism 1.2 (September 2010). (Poem)<br>229.    “The Magical Charm of Rabindranath Tagore`s Short Stories: An Evaluation.” In <br>Muse India (E-journal) Issue 33, September-October 2010.  (Research Article)<br>230.    “A Feminist Reading of Kamala Das`s Poetry” in Karisma of Kamala Das. Ed. Chandra Mouli. New Delhi: GNOSIS, 2010. (Critical Article)<br>231.    “Nissim Ezekiel: The Father of Modernity in Indian English Poetry” in Discourses in Contemporary Indian English Poets. Ed. K. V. Dominic. New Delhi: GNOSIS, 2010. (Critical Article)<br>232.    “The Conflict between Spirituality and Materialism in R. K. Narayan`s The Vendor of Sweets” in Poetcrit Vol. XXIII July 2010 No. 2. (Research Article)<br>233.    “Who is Responsible?” in IJPCL 10.1 (June 2010). (Short Story)<br>234.    “Acceptance, Rejection, Compromise: Three One Act Play (A Trilogy by Pronab Kumar Majumder” in Labyrinth: The Biannual Literary Journal of Postmodernism 1.1 (March 2010). (Review)<br>235.    “Who is Responsible?” in Muse India (E-journal) Issue 30 (March-April 2010). (Short Story)<br>236.    “Stephen Gill`s The Flame: An Epic on Anti-terrorism” in The Flame Unmasked: Stephen Gill`s Epic Critically Examined. Ed. Sudhir K. Arora. Bareilly: Prakash Book Depot, 2010. (Critical Article)<br>237.    “A Cow on the Lane” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Jan. 2010. (Poem)<br>238.    “Magic Realism and New Historicism in Salman Rushdie`s Midnight`s Children” in Seva Bharati Journal of English Studies 6 (January 2010). (Critical Article)<br>239.    “Teresa`s Tears” in Seva Bharati Journal of English Studies 6 (January 2010). (Poem)<br>240.    “Wolfgang, the Messiah of Nature” in Bridge-in-Making Number 55 &  56 (January 2010). (Poem)<br>241.    “A Good Samaritan” in the IJPCL Vol. 9 No. 2 (December 2009). (Short Story)<br>242.    “Celibacy,” “Beauty,” “Gayatri`s Solitude,” “A Nightmare” and A Blissful Voyage” in Kohinoor Vol. 6 No. 2 (2009). (Poems)<br>243.    “A Blissful Voyage,” “A Nightmare,” “I am Just a Mango Tree,” and “Work is Worship” in MuseIndia (E-journal) Issue 28 (November-December 2009). (Poems)<br>244.    “Nature`s Bounties” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Nov. 2009. (Poem)<br>245.    “The Conflict between Tradition and Modernity in R. K. Narayan`s The Guide” in Parnassus: An Innovative Journal of Literary Criticism. Jun 2009. (Critical Study)<br>246.    “A Nightmare” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Jul. 2009. (Poem)<br>247.    “Blissful Voyage” and “Gods will be Pleased” in Posy of Poesy, edited by P. Gopichand and Nagasuseela. Guntur: P. Gopichand and Nagasuseela, 2009. (Poems)<br>248.    “An Email from Senthilkumar” in Bridge-in-Making  Number 53&54 (July 2009). (Short Story)<br>249.    “A Nightmare,” “Laxmi`s Plea” and “Nature`s Bounties” in the IJPCL Vol. 9 No. 1 (June 2009). (Poems)<br>250.    “R. K. Narayan`s The Guide: A Postcolonial Reading” in Postcolonial Readings in Indo-Anglian Literature. Ed. K. V. Dominic. New Delhi: Authors Press, 2009. (Research Study)<br>251.    “The Twins” (Short Story) in Selected Short Stories in Contemporary Indo-Anglian Literature, Edited by K. V. Dominic,  published by Sarup Book Publishers, New Delhi, 2009. <br>252.    “The Conflict between Tradition and Modernity in R. K. Narayan`s The Vendor of Sweets” in The Journal of Contemporary Literature, Vol. 1 No. 1 Jan. 2009 (Research Article)<br>253.    “A Blissful Voyage” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Feb. 2009. (Poem)<br>254.    “I am Just a Mango Tree” Kerala Private College Teacher.  Jan. 2009. (Poem)<br>255.    “Sleepless Nights,” “Harvest Feast,” and “Lal Salaam to Labourers” in the IJPCL Jan-Jun 2008. (Poems)<br>256.    “Work is Worship” in Poetcrit Vol. XXI July 2008 No. 2.<br>257.    “I am Just a Mango Tree” in the IJPCL Jul-Dec 2008. (Poem)<br>258.    “Doris Lessing and The Golden Note Book” in the IJPCL Jul-Dec 2007 (Critical Study)<br>259.    “Celibacy” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Sept. 2007. (Poem)<br>260.    “Beauty” in the IJPCL Jan-Jun 2007. (Poem)<br>261.    “Gods will be Pleased” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Feb. 2007. (Poem)<br>262.    “Haves and Have-nots” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Nov. 2006. (Poem)<br>263.    “Towering Personalities of Womanhood: A Reading of Tagore`s Heroines in Short Stories” in the IJPCL Jan-Dec 2006. (Research Article)<br>264.    “What shall I Pray?” and “Tsunami Camps” in the IJPCL Jan-Dec 2004. (Poems)<br>265.    “Long Live E. K. Nayanar” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Jul. 2004. (Poem)<br>266.    “In Memoriam George Joson” in Kerala Private College Teacher.  Jun. 2004. (Poem)<br>267.    “Fanon`s The Wretched of the Earth: A Saga of the Colonized” in the IJPCL Jan-Dec 2002. (Book review and critical analysis)<br>268.    “Rasa in Tagore`s “The Postmaster”` in the IJPCL July 2001. (Theoretical application)<br>269.    “Realism in Tagore`s Stories” in the IJPCL July 2000. (Critical Study)<br><br><br><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">INTERVIEWS WITH K. V. DOMINIC</span><br> <br>1.    Interview with Prof. Dr. K. V. Dominic by Prof. Elisabetta Marino published in MOSAICO, Italiano February 2012 issue and English Translation in Labyrinth Vol-4 No.-2, Gwalior, India, April, 2012.<br>2.    Interview with Prof. K. V. Dominic by Dr. Rohit Phutela published in International Journal on Multicultural Literature, Vol-7, No-1, Kerala, India, January, 2017.<br>3.    Prof. K. V. Dominic in Conversation with Parthajit Ghosh published in Poetcrit  Vol-30, No-2, Himachal Pradesh, India, july 2017<br>4.    Revelations of a Pantheistic Poet: Dr K.V. Dominic in Conversation  by Goutam Karmakar published in Writers in Conversation, Flinders University Journal, Vol-4, No- 2, Australia, August 2017. <br>   5.   Interview with Dr. K. V. Dominic by Dr. Jernail Singh Anand. Published in Interviews with Poets, Critical Thinkers and Scholars, 21st CENTURY CRITICAL THOUGHT, A DIALOGUE WITH POSTMODERN VOICES,Volume 3. By Jernail Singh Anand. Published by Earth Vision Publications, Gurugram, Haryana, India, 2021.<br> 6.    Barathi, Dr. S., editor. A Conversation with Contemporary Indian English Poets. Book Leaf Publishing, 2023. <br><br><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">PhD Researches on K V Dominic`s Poetry</span><br><br>1. Dr. Gautam Karmakar, Assistant Professor of English at Barabazar Bikram Tudu Memorial College, Sidho-Kanho-Birsha University, West Bengal, on the topic “Social realism in the select poems of five contemporary post-independence Indian poets in English, namely Keki N. Daruwalla, Shiv K. Kumar, T. V. Reddy, Pashupati Jha and K. V. Dominic” at NIT, Durgapur, West Bengal. He was awarded PhD on 29th February 2020.<br>2. Ramya Kalaivani K. under Dr. Raichel M Sylus in Avinashilingam Institute for Home Science and Higher Education for Women, Coimbatore on the topic Tinai in the poetry of K V Dominic<br>3. H. M. Devakumar, on Eco-critical aspects in the selected poems of Dr. K. V. Raghupathy and K. V. Dominic at Gitam University, Bangalore campus, under the guidance of Dr. P. Sreeramulu.<br>4. Kalaivanan, Asst. Prof. English, Thiruvalluvar Govt. Arts College, Rasipuram on Social Concerns in the poetry of T. V. Reddy, I. K. Sharma and K. V. Dominic, under the guidance of Dr. S. Kumaran.<br>5. J. Priyanka, Pondicherry University, on Nature in the poetry of North Eastern and South Indian poets including Mamang Dai, T. V. Reddy, K. V. Raghupathy, P. Raja, Parthasarathy, Kamala Das, K. V. Dominic, A. K. Ramanujan,  etc.<br><br><br><br>K. V. Dominic as Secretary of Guild of Indian English Writers Editors and Critics (GIEWEC)<br>A literary forum of Indian writers in English, entitled Guild of Indian English Writers, Editors and Critics (GIEWEC) was established on 24 September 2010 at Thodupuzha, Kerala, India. It was registered on 13 October 2010 and its Head Office is functioning at the residence of the Secretary at Thodupuzha. GIEWEC is a non-profit-making institution aimed at promoting Indian literature in English through the members` creative and critical writings and by editing and publishing books and journals. The chief objectives of the Guild are: to promote Indian Literature in English, to exchange creative writings between nations, to conduct annual conferences and literary festivals, and to help Ph.D. scholars. The Guild is publishing an international biannual refereed journal entitled Writers Editors Critics (ISSN 2231-198X). The issues come out very punctually in March and September. The journal`s Volume 15 Number 2 September 2025 issue was published in August 2025. GIEWEC is fast growing with more than 200 members now of which 92 are life (patron) members. Professors and Lecturers of universities/colleges, School teachers, professional English writers and PhD research scholars are its members. In addition to the publication of the journal, GIEWEC organizes international/national literature conferences in various cities of India in collaboration with universities and colleges. So far it has organized such nine conferences. GIEWEC has helped several members and non-members in getting published their critical and creative books. It also conducts workshops on Research Methodology in several colleges across the country. Similarly hundreds of research scholars have been guided in writing their theses by the members of GIEWEC. For more details kindly visit its web site: www.giewecindia.com<br><br></div>', '', 1);
