(13, 'Critical Studies on Contemporary Indian English Women Writers (Edited Book)', '<span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">K. V. Dominics (ed) Critical Studies on Contemporary Indian English Women Writers. New Delhi: Sarup Book Publishers Pvt. Ltd., 2010. HB. Pp. xx+336. Rs. 1100. ISBN-978-81-7625-631-5.<br><br>Contents<br><br>Editors Preface<br><br>The Stone Angel: The Story of a Woman with a Will<br><br>“Basavaraj Naikar<br><br>Postmodernism as Feminist Text: A Reading of the Short Text of Canadian Women Writers<br><br>“Jameela Begum A.<br><br>Assertion of the Female Self through Motherhood in Margaret Atwoods Surfacing<br><br>“Rajeshwar Mittapalli & A. Aravind Reddy<br><br>œAlone Alone All All Alone: The Loneliness of Almeda Roth in Alice Munros œMeneseteung<br><br>“K. Radha<br><br>E. J. Pratts Historic Canadian Perception of Mankind in Select Poems<br><br>“G. Baskaran <br><br>Aboriginality and Ritas Sisters: A Cursory Glance at the Native Theatre in Canada<br><br>“B. Chandrika<br><br>From Society through Sex to Selfhood: Attainment of Identity in Margaret Laurences A Jest of God<br><br>“Rajeshwar Mittapalli & A. Aravind Reddy<br><br>Rohinton Mistrys Tales from Firozsha Baag: A Vista of Memories and Fantasies<br><br>“Evangeline Shanti Roy<br><br>Indian Diasporic Writers in Canada: A Birds Eye View<br><br>“Satendra Kumar<br><br>œThe Marginal Men in Stephen Gills Immigrant and Uma  Parameswarans Rootless But Green are the Boulevard Trees<br><br>“G. Dominic Savio & S. J. Kala<br><br>Interview with Dr. Stephen Gill<br><br>“K. V. Dominic<br><br>The Mega Text in Margaret Atwoods The Handmaids Tale<br><br>“Hema Nair R.<br><br>Bending Time and Space: Margaret Laurences A Bird in the House and Margaret Atwoods Cats Eye<br><br>“Dolors Collellmir<br><br>What Man has Made of Man: Portrayal of the Marginalised ˜Red Children and Subversion of the Deceptive Myths in Sharon Pollocks Docudrama Walsh<br><br>“S. Chandralekha<br><br>The Schizophrenic World of Jewish-Canadian Poetry<br><br>“Rajasekhar Patteti<br><br>Margaret Atwoods Weltanschauung<br><br>“Mariamma Chacko<br><br>Margaret Atwood: Twenty-Five years of Gothic Tales<br><br>“Behzad Pourgharib<br><br>John Ralston Saul, Cultural Globalization and Canadian Realities<br><br>“Motaleb Azari<br><br>Sexual Power Politics: A Post-Modern Reading of Margaret Atwoods The Edible Woman and Toni Morrisons Sula<br><br>“W. S. Kottiswari<br><br>In Defiance of the Plague of Perfection: Timothy Findleys The Butterfly Plague<br><br> “Achamma Alex <br><br>Postmodernist Narrative Strategies in Margaret Atwoods Alias Grace<br><br>“Lata Mishra<br><br>Economic Hardships and Exploitation of Lower Caste Woman in the Fiction of Rohinton Mistry<br><br>“Manoj Kumar<br><br>Inner Journey of the Immigrant Protagonist in Margaret Laurences The Fire Dwellers: A Psychological Perspective<br><br>”Elizabeth Lucy<br><br>List of Contributors<br><br>Index</span>', '1', '83715fd41083.png', '', 1),
(14, 'Discourses on Contemporary Indian English Poets (Edited Book)ts', '<span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">K. V. Dominics (ed) Discourses on Contemporary Indian English Poets. New Delhi: GNOSIS, 2010. HB. Pp. x+221. Rs. 600. ISBN 978-81-89012-82-7.<br><br> <br><br>Contents<br><br>Editors Preface<br><br>Indian English Poetry after 1960: Some Reflections<br><br>-P C K Prem<br><br>Nissim Ezekiel: The Father of Modernity in Indian English Poetry<br><br>-K. V. Dominic<br><br>Culturally Speaking: Nissim Ezekiel<br><br>-Dhishna P<br><br>Swallows Fly Tame: Kamala Dass Journey toWomanhood<br><br>-Hyacinth Pink<br><br>Feminism and Kamala Das<br><br>-Shubha Mukherjee<br><br>Jayanta Mahapatra: A True Bard to His Roots<br><br>-Satendra Kumar<br><br>The Still Sad Music of [Indianity]: Pluralist Identity and Socio-cultural Non Specificity in Jayanta Mahapatras Random Descent<br><br>-S. K. Prathap<br><br>Syed Ameeruddins Poetry in Quest of the Beyond<br><br>-Aju Mukhopadhyay<br><br>Vision and Verve: Syed Ameeruddins Visioned Summits and Visions of Deliverance<br><br>-Masoodul Hassan<br><br>The Paradigm of Paradox: A Study of Shiv K. Kumars Losing My Way<br><br>-D. C. Chambial<br><br>Erotic Consciousness in the Poetry of Shiv K. Kumar and R. K. Singh: A Comparative Study<br><br>-Jindagi Kumari & Rajni Singh<br><br>Mothers Lap by IK Sharma: A Study in Text<br><br>-D. C. Chambial<br><br>Towards Maturation and Crystallization: A Study of D. C. Chambials Poetry<br><br>-V.V.B Rama Rao<br><br>Odyssey of Time in the Poetry of Pronab Kumar Majumder<br><br>-P. V. Laxmi Prasad<br><br>Portrait of an Artist as a Poet: Grasping the Grammar of Dilip Chitre As is Where is<br><br>“Hemang A. Desai<br><br>Philosophical Flavour in Manas Bakshis The Midnight Star<br><br>-P. V. Laxmi Prasad<br><br>Aju Mukhopadhyays Poetry: Spectra of Nature, Beauty and Spirituality<br><br>-Shujaat Hussain<br><br>Universality of Hazara Singhs Poems<br><br>-S. Kumaran<br><br>List of Contributors</span>', '1', 'f629ed931094.png', '', 1),
(15, 'Discourses on Five Indian Poets in English (Edited Book)', '<span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">K. V. Dominics (ed) Discourses on Five Indian Poets in English: Keki N. Daruwalla, Shiv K. Kumar, Pronab Kumar Majumder, Syed Ameeruddin and Aju Mukhopadhyay. New Delhi: Authorspress, 2011. HB. Pp. xv + 311. Rs. 825. ISBN 978-81-7273-603-3.<br><br> CONTENTS<br><br>Preface<br><br>Poetry of Shiv K. Kumar, Keki N. Daruwalla, Syed Ameeruddin, Aju Mukhopadhyay and Pronab Kumar Majumder: Some Reflections<br><br>-P C K  PREM<br><br>Landscapes: A Landmark in Keki Daruwallas Poetry<br><br>“Asha Viswas<br><br>A Study of Discourse Construction in the Select Poem of Keki N. Daruwalla<br><br>“Jayshree Goswami and Dr Mojibur Rahman<br><br>Policing the Poet: An Ecocritical Study of Daruwalas Nature Poems<br><br>“P. V. Jayaraj<br><br>Some Reflections on the Poetry of Keki N. Daruwalla<br><br>-P  C K  PREM<br><br>Keki N. Daruwallas Poetry: An Eco-critical Perspective<br><br>“Prasenjit Das & Binita Sarma<br><br>The Sounds of Starved Eros: A Study of Shiv K. Kumars Thus Spake the Buddha<br><br>-D. C. Chambial<br><br>Nostalgic Past and Agonized Present in the Poetry of Shiv K. Kumar<br><br>“P C K  PREM<br><br>The East-West Fusion in Shiv K. Kumars Poetry<br><br>-Anshu Bhardwaj<br><br>Searching Peace and Harmony in an Age of Terror: A Study of Pronab Kumar Majumders Passage to Peace<br><br>“P C K PREM<br><br>Time “ The Dominant Theme of Pronab Kumar Majumders Poetry.<br><br>“Kurt F. Svatek<br><br>Minor Themes Vis-a-vis Time in Pronab Kumar Majumders Poetry<br><br>“Swagata Ray, R. K. Singh & M. Mojibur Rahman<br><br>Pronab  Kumar Majumder: An Adept in Love Poems<br><br>“Aju Mukhopadhyay<br><br>Critical Perspectives on Prof. Syed Ameeruddins Poetry<br><br>-Binayendra Chowdhuri<br><br>Quest for Self: The Poetry of Syed Ameeruddin<br><br>“D. C. Chambial<br><br>A Critical Analysis of the Poetry of Prof.  Syed Ameeruddln.<br><br>“Har Prasad Sharma<br><br>Syed Ameeruddin as a Didactic Poet<br><br>“S. Kumaran<br><br>Syed Ameeruddin: A Wanderer with a Goal<br><br>“ Mosam Sinha<br><br>Syed Ameeruddins Poetry: Visions of a Pilgrimage to the Summits of Deliverance<br><br>“O. P. Mathur<br><br>An Elevating Spiritual Journey of Grace and Nobility through Syed Ameeruddins Visioned Summits and Visions of Deliverance<br><br>“P C K Prem<br><br>Poetic Style in Syed Ameeruddins Poetry<br><br>“Rajeev Ranjan, R. K. Singh, M. Mojibur Rahman<br><br>Aju Mukhopadhyay: Voice of the Voiceless<br><br>“S. Kumaran<br><br>Purifying the Contemporary Inherent Irritants: A Critical appreciation of Aju Mukhopadhyays Poems on Sri Aurobindo and the   Mother and The Paper Boat<br><br>-P C K Prem<br><br>An Ecoaesthetic Rendering of Aju Mukhopadhyays Poems<br><br>“Raj Sree M. S.</span>', '1', 'ae1eaa321032.png', '', 1),
(16, 'Pathos in the Short Stories of Rabindranath Tagore (Research Book)', '<span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">K. V. Dominics Pathos in the Short Stories of Rabindranath Tagore. New Delhi: Sarup Book Publishers Pvt. Ltd., 2009. Hard Bound. ISBN-978-81-7625-923-1. x+140 pp. Rs. 450.<br><br> <br><br>CONTENTS<br><br> Introduction<br><br>Short Stories with Pathos of Women<br><br>Short Stories with Pathos of Men  <br><br>Short Stories with Pathos of Children<br><br>Conclusion<br><br>Works Cited</span>', '1', 'f5c150af1098.png', '', 1),
(17, 'Postcolonial Readings in Indo-Anglian Literature (Edited Book)', '<h2 align=\"left\" style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px; font-size: 22px; font-family: Georgia, \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-style:=\"\" italic;=\"\" color:=\"\" rgb(51,=\"\" 51,=\"\" 51);\"=\"\">K. V. Dominics (ed.) <em style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">Postcolonial Readings in Indo-Anglian Literature</em>. New Delhi, Authors Press, 2009. Hard Bound. ISBN 978-81-7273-479-4. xiii+306 pp. Rs. 800.</h2><p align=\"center\" style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">CONTENTS</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">1</strong><span style=\"font-weight: bold;\">.</span>Vive la Difference! Imperialism, Colonialism, Post-Colonialism:</p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\">Challenges to Cultural Plurality</p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">” K. Ayyappa Paniker</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">2.</strong>Beyond New Literatures and Post Coloniality</p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">” Sreedevi K. Nair</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">3.</strong>Postcoloniality as Counter-Discourse<strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\"></strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">” E. A. Thomas</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">4.</strong>  Women: Colonial Narratives and Post-Colonial Images<strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\"></strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">” </strong><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">G. S. Jayasree</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">5.</strong>The Short Story Genre, Postcolonialism and Women Writers<strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\"></strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">  ” H. Kalpana</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">6.</strong> The Tragic Dilemma of Larins Sahib</p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"> <strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">Basavaraj Naikar</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">7</strong>.  <em style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">Latter-day Psalms</em>: A Post-Colonial Reading</p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">” Fed Mathew</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">8.</strong>Post-colonial Imaging of Human Suffering in PCK Prems <em style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">Oracles of the Last  Decade</em></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; padding: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: \" times=\"\" new=\"\" roman\",=\"\" times,=\"\" serif;=\"\" font-size:=\"\" 15px;\"=\"\"><strong style=\"margin: 0px; padding: 0px;\">” D. C. Chambial</strong></p><p style=\"margin-top: 1.5em; margin-bottom: 1.5em; paddi